<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Spec 31 §3/§5: the macOS runtime signing profile (pinned here until
  spec 31 §2's manifest derivation lands).

  allow-dyld-environment-variables — THE LOAD-BEARING GRANT
    (tebako-runtime-r#5). The spec 22 tier-1 visibility design re-execs
    the exe with the driver-armed DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES (the in-image
    libtfs_preload.dylib serves the VFS to R's UNPATCHED libc IO). The
    hardened runtime strips every DYLD_* variable at launch unless this
    grant is present; the second incarnation then runs shimless, the
    mount root (/__tfs__) does not exist on the host filesystem, and R
    dies "cannot find system Renviron" then "unable to open the base
    package" — while the version fast path keeps answering (it never
    touches R), which is exactly issue #5's symptom. Proven against the
    published v0.1.0 pair and by the fixed-bytes A/B (same exe, same
    image, only this grant added → green). The macos-15 runners
    TOLERATE the omission (their dyld keeps the variables), so no CI
    runner can police this class: ci/macos-sign-notarize-runtime.sh
    asserts the embedded profile statically, by key name.
  disable-library-validation — a hardened-runtime process by default
    refuses unsigned dylibs, and the driver's boot materializes in-image
    native objects (the preload shim, the lapack module,
    libRlapack/libRblas) to the host for dlopen; none carries a
    Developer ID signature of its own (§1.4). This grant relaxes WHICH
    dylibs may load — it says nothing about WHETHER dyld honors
    environment variables; only the grant above does (the §3 pair's
    original comment conflated the two).
  allow-jit — a grant, never a requirement: R has no JIT surface (the
    byte compiler is an ahead-of-time serializer), so the entitlement
    is inert today; it rides the profile so the profile never needs a
    re-review if a JIT-bearing interpreter configuration arrives.

  Keep each <true/> on its own line: the embedding check in
  ci/macos-sign-notarize-runtime.sh counts them AND greps each key name
  (macOS 15 emits the embedded plist single-line).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
